1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:10,792
Slide 17 diagrams the "ASYNCHRONOUS ENGINE: THE 3-LAYER SPARK PIPELINE." This is the core software architecture of our lab.

2
00:00:10,792 --> 00:00:15,570
Look at the three interconnected layers displayed on screen.

3
00:00:16,470 --> 00:00:30,680
Examine Layer 1 on the left: "LAYER 1: TRIGGER & SENSING." This layer handles incoming stimuli—cron timer heartbeats, incoming Gmail webhooks, GitHub push events, or Google Drive file uploads.

4
00:00:30,680 --> 00:00:38,600
It normalizes raw HTTP payloads, validates HMAC signatures, and pushes tasks into an in-memory event queue.

5
00:00:39,500 --> 00:00:46,952
Now look at Layer 2 in the center: "LAYER 2: ASYNC EXECUTION ENGINE." This is where the computational work occurs.

6
00:00:46,952 --> 00:00:52,574
A decoupled worker pool pops events from the queue and feeds them to Gemini 3.5 Flash.

7
00:00:52,574 --> 00:01:01,270
If a tool call fails or an external API times out, Layer 2 executes exponential backoff retries without blocking the main event loop!

8
00:01:02,170 --> 00:01:18,337
And look at Layer 3 on the right: "LAYER 3: AUDIT & NOTIFICATION." Every single state mutation, tool invocation, and decision is cryptographically signed with SHA-256 and appended to an immutable SQLite audit trail.

9
00:01:18,337 --> 00:01:26,910
Only when the entire pipeline succeeds does it dispatch a concise 1-page executive summary to the human architect.

10
00:01:27,810 --> 00:01:33,489
In traditional single-threaded scripts, an API network timeout crashes your entire program.

11
00:01:33,489 --> 00:01:42,040
In our 3-Layer Spark Pipeline, Layer 1 keeps collecting events, Layer 2 isolates failures safely, and Layer 3 guarantees audit integrity!

12
00:01:42,940 --> 00:01:49,520
This strict separation of concerns is what gives our avatars enterprise-grade reliability and resilience.

